---
name: dual-agent-review
description: >-
  Run a two-phase Claude + Codex review loop in Orca: plan then code, with
  Grok support work and read-only Codex review. Claude self-reviews first;
  at most 3 rounds per phase, then notify the coordinator or human.
  Use when starting a feature worktree, asking Claude to plan or implement
  and Codex to review, bootstrapping with orca-kit-init, or when the user
  mentions dual-agent-review or plan/code review rounds.
---

# dual-agent-review

Reusable Orca workflow: **one worktree per feature**, **Claude plans and implements**, **Grok supports**, **Codex reviews read-only**.

Follow [templates/AGENTS.md](templates/AGENTS.md), or the initialized project's `AGENTS.md` for project commands and overrides. Existing project instructions remain in force. A project `Roles` section can override role assignments; substitute those assignments wherever the loop names default roles below.

## Roles

- **Claude**: substantive planning, implementation, and self-review before every Codex review.
- **Grok**: local `grok` CLI, available through **2026-11-30**. Handle research, doc reading, test/build execution, log summaries, PR drafts, and process-file updates. Send Claude concise results; do not redo support work in Claude.
- **Codex reviewer**: strictly read-only (`codex exec --sandbox read-only`); inspect code and test evidence, return a verdict as output. The writable caller persists it.
- **Fallback**: when Grok is unavailable, including from **2026-12-01**, use a separate **Codex support invocation** for chores and necessary test/process-file writes. The review invocation remains read-only. Claude retains core work. Use one writer at a time; if delegation is unavailable or unauthorized, the implementer handles chores.
- **bobo (human)**: approves the plan before coding (except Fast path), reviews the diff before merge, and decides after 3 unresolved rounds.

## Feature-local files

All plans, state, tasks, handoffs, escalation notes, and test logs live in **`.agents/work/<slug>/`** for a unique feature slug. Short process filenames below refer to this directory. Shared instructions `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `CODEX_REVIEW.md` stay at the root.

Initialization adds the [ignore fragment](templates/work.gitignore) to `.gitignore`. Ignoring `/.agents/work/` avoids a manual deletion step before every merge. Never force-add process files. Check `git ls-files -- .agents/work` before merge; remove any previously tracked process files from the index on the feature branch, keeping local copies.

## Loop

Two phases: **plan**, then **code**. At most **3 rounds per phase**, fewer when sufficient; never start a 4th.

1. Claude writes the plan/code and self-reviews. The support agent records decisions in `TASKS.md`, `STATE.md` (`phase`, `round: N/3`), and `HANDOFF_REQUEST.md`.
2. In code rounds, the implementer or support agent runs tests and build/lint in a writable environment **before review**, attaching exact commands, runner, cwd, revision/diff identity, timestamps, exit codes, log paths, and summaries to the handoff.
3. Codex reviews read-only and returns the [verdict template](templates/HANDOFF_VERDICT.md). The caller checks process exit and format, then saves `HANDOFF_VERDICT.md` and updates state. Verdict is `APPROVE` or `CHANGES`; grades are `blocker`, `major`, `minor`. Only blocker/major findings (including failed/blocked required checks) require another round.
4. After plan approval by Codex, wait for bobo's explicit approval before coding (approval already in the task counts). Record approver and date in state.
5. Code-round done: tests pass, build or lint passes, Codex says `APPROVE`. Codex checks logs against the reviewed code, not write-producing builds in the read-only sandbox. Actual failing checks are `FAIL`; only missing or suspicious evidence is `BLOCKED`. The sandbox's inability to build alone is not a blocker.
6. If necessary, the implementer/support agent reruns in a disposable writable copy of the exact reviewed tree, including intended uncommitted changes. Document copy creation, source revision/diff identity, copy path, environment setup, commands, exits, and logs. Codex reads that evidence; do not switch the reviewer sandbox.
7. After 3 unresolved rounds, stop, write `ESCALATION.md`, **and notify**: dispatched workers use `orchestration ask` for decisions or `send --type escalation` with current Task/Dispatch IDs when blocked; end the turn. Non-workers use `worktree set --unread --workspace-status in-review`. See [shared notification commands](templates/AGENTS.md#escalation-approvals-and-completion).
8. Worker completion goes through `send --type worker_done` with current IDs and truthful `--outcome`, exactly once; follow printed recovery on failure, end the turn on confirmation. Do not replace worker messaging with unread markers.
9. Before each code round, checkpoint on the feature branch (WIP commit of intended files, or HEAD SHA if clean). Never commit to main/master or commit secrets; leave the diff for bobo before merge.

**Fast path**: about 20 lines or fewer and no design/API/schema/protocol/security/dependency/new-feature impact may skip plan review. Record the reason in state. If design impact emerges, open the plan phase and notify via the same worker/non-worker channel.

Use [HANDOFF_REQUEST](templates/HANDOFF_REQUEST.md) and [HANDOFF_VERDICT](templates/HANDOFF_VERDICT.md) headings so the handoff stays parseable. Detailed review rules and caller-side output capture are in [CODEX_REVIEW](templates/CODEX_REVIEW.md).

## Bootstrap

```sh
/home/xai/orca-agent-kit/bin/orca-kit-init /path/to/repo feature-slug
```

The optional slug defaults to `feature`; pass a distinct slug for each parallel feature. Initialization appends a marked rule block to existing `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` once, preserves their original contents, and appends the ignore block once. Other existing files are skipped, including each feature's process files. Marked blocks are not automatically upgraded; inspect and update them deliberately when installing a later kit version.

Fill in project test/build commands in `AGENTS.md` and the slug/branch fields in newly copied process templates. Work on `feat/<slug>` or an Orca feature worktree. The script does not commit anything; keep process files out of git. For old installations, move root process files into the chosen feature directory and untrack them on the feature branch; initialization never migrates or deletes them. See the Chinese [README](README.md) for daily use.

## Bundled files

- `templates/AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX_REVIEW.md` — shared rules, implementer entry, reviewer checklist
- `templates/PLAN.md`, `TASKS.md`, `STATE.md`, `HANDOFF_REQUEST.md`, `HANDOFF_VERDICT.md`, `ESCALATION.md` — copied into each feature directory
- `templates/work.gitignore` — local process-directory ignore block
- `bin/orca-kit-init` — idempotent bootstrap

